+# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
+# [MP8] Multi-device support (2026-08-17)
+# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
+#
+# **Retirement** is a rule the user set — for the 4th person killed by a device, the device **doesn't come off them** after the kill
+# and leaves rotation for good (a corpse carrying a device stays on screen). After that only one device is left on the field,
+# which guarantees the last two players get a "one device passed back and forth" showdown instead of each carrying one and both dying together.
+# The actual skipped-reset behavior lives in break_spine_state.gd. This only stores the flag.
+var mp8_retired: bool = false
+
+# Index, only so the logs are readable (1 = the original device in the scene, 2 = the second one this mod adds)
+var mp8_index: int = 1
+
+# [MP8] Speed baseline [default_speed, new_target_speed, speed_increase_over_time],
+# recorded once by the minigame on the first scaling. After that it's always "baseline × factor" —
+# devices are reused across rounds while the speed-setting code runs every round, so multiplying the current value by 1.2 two rounds in a row gives 1.44.
+var mp8_speed_base: Array = []
+
+# [MP8] This device's body color, set by the minigame in mp8_ensure_devices_rpc (set once on each peer).
+# On attach it has to be carried over to the visual on the grabbed player's back, so the device needs to remember it.
+# [MP8] The retired device's **light color** (requested by the user on 2026-08-30 — a retired device shows a green light).
+#
+# ⚠️ A previous version got this wrong once by repainting the **body texture** (albedo). The user pointed out right away that they wanted
+#    "the blinking light" to turn green, not the device painted green — noted here so nobody changes it back.
+#
+# The light on the player's back is `spine_breaker_player.spine_breaker_light` (OmniLight3D),
+# which is set to `Color.RED` when the device latches on (see spine_breaker_attached_rpc in the player script).
+# **The device's own `status_light` can't be seen** — as soon as it attaches it goes invisible and is moved 100 m underground.
+#
+# Why green? In this minigame green has always meant "this device isn't a threat"
+# (`spine_broken_exited_rpc` shows a green light after a reset), and retired = permanently out of rotation, so the meaning matches.
+# Before this, retiring **turned the light off**, leaving a pitch-black lump on the corpse's back, and players couldn't tell whether that device would move again —
+# which is exactly what they most need to know. How many devices are still hunting on the field directly decides whether to keep running.
+const MP8_RETIRE_LIGHT: Color = Color(0.2, 1.0, 0.3)
+
+# [MP8] The retired device's blink speed (requested by the user on 2026-08-30 — keep blinking even after retiring).
+#
+# It uses a **fixed slow speed** rather than keeping the fuse speed. Normal blink speed = how much fuse is left
+# (`1.0 + (1 - remaining_ratio) * 3.0` in `_process`, blinking faster the closer it gets to the execution).
+# At the moment of retirement the fuse has just burned out, so the speed is stuck at its fastest — leaving it as is would give
+# "a dead device blinking frantically on a corpse", which reads like it's about to strike at any moment, the exact opposite.
+# 0.6 is even slower than the base speed, an "idle pulse" rhythm, and only makes sense paired with green. **It's still lit, but in no hurry.**
+const MP8_RETIRE_BLINK: float = 0.6
+
+var mp8_tint: Color = Color.WHITE
+
+# [MP8] The part of the body texture name "after the last underscore" (measured in practice = "spinebreaker base1.png").
+# Used to recognize "which surface is the device" among the meshes on the player's back — both sides are different copies of the same texture.
+# Read live from the device's own material instead of hard-coding the string.
+var mp8_tex_key: String = ""
+
