+# [MP8] Turn off the retired device's light (2026-08-17, user tested in practice "the stopped one is still blinking").
+#
+# Why not just reuse spine_broken_exited_rpc? It also sets `visible = true`,
+# which would drag the retired device (meant to stay 100 m underground) back into view — the whole visual point of retiring is
+# "the device stays on the corpse's back", so the device itself has to stay invisible. Hence a separate RPC that only turns off the light.
+#
+# ⚠️ What's actually blinking is **the device visual on the corpse's back**, not this device itself (which is invisible).
+# The player's _process does `blink_animation_player.speed_scale =
+# this_device.light_animation_player.speed_scale` every frame, and nobody stops the retired device's light.
+# So `speed_scale = 0` here is **the load-bearing line** — it follows the existing sync chain
+# to the corpse on every client automatically. Calling stop() directly on the corpse's copy is a double safety net,
+# since `target.blink_animation_player.stop()` in `break_spine_state.enter()`
+# only ran on the side running the state machine (the server), and the clients' copies were never stopped.
+@rpc("any_peer", "call_local", "reliable")
+func mp8_retire_quiet_rpc(victim_path: NodePath) -> void :
+
+	if not NetworkManager.mp8_sender_is_host(multiplayer):
+		return
+
+	activation_timer.stop()
+	light_animation_player.stop()
+	# [MP8] ⚠️ This speed_scale is **not** for the device itself, it's for the player.
+	# Every frame the player's `_process` syncs its own `blink_animation_player.speed_scale`
+	# to **this device value**. So to keep the light on the corpse's back blinking, this line is the only knob —
+	# setting it to 0 means "no blinking", which is also why the light didn't blink in the previous version.
+	# And the device's `_process` only overwrites it while `activation_timer` isn't stopped. Retiring already stopped the timer,
+	# so the value set here won't get overwritten.
+	light_animation_player.speed_scale = MP8_RETIRE_BLINK
+	# [MP8] This used to be Color.BLACK (light off). By now the device itself is invisible and has been moved 100 m underground,
+	# so this line is basically never seen — what actually makes players see green is the tint on the back visual below.
+	# It's changed to green here too just for consistency (if the device itself ever becomes visible again, it shouldn't be black).
+	status_light.light_color = Color.GREEN
+
+	var v = get_node_or_null(victim_path)
+	if v is SpineBreakerPlayer and v.blink_animation_player != null:
+
+		# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
+		# [MP8] 🩸 It has to be **explicitly replayed**, just "not calling stop" isn't enough (user tested on 2026-08-30 and the light never came on at all)
+		# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
+		#
+		# The previous version used `stop()` here, and players reported "the light never even turned on". Two causes stacked together.
+		#
+		#   ① `AnimationPlayer.stop()` leaves the animated properties **at whatever frame they were on**.
+		#      The blink animation has an "off" phase, and stopping on it → the light's brightness is 0,
+		#      **so no change to `light_color` will be visible** (black times any color is still black).
+		#   ② The server and clients were already inconsistent. In `break_spine_state.enter()`, the line
+		#      `target.blink_animation_player.stop()` **only ran on the side running the state machine (the server)**,
+		#      and the clients' copy was never stopped. So if we merely "stop calling stop here",
+		#      the host sees a dark light while clients still see it blinking — even worse.
+		#
+		# So here we **actively start playing it**, so both sides start from the same state.
+		var ap: AnimationPlayer = v.blink_animation_player
+		var anim: String = ap.current_animation
+		if anim == "" and ap.get_animation_list().size() > 0:
+			anim = ap.get_animation_list()[0]
+		if anim != "":
+			ap.play(anim)
+		ap.speed_scale = MP8_RETIRE_BLINK
+
+	# [MP8] Turn the light on the corpse's back green (requested by the user on 2026-08-30).
+	#
+	# Together with the two spots above, the result is **a slowly blinking green light** (the user decided on 2026-08-30 that it keeps blinking after retiring).
+	# Red = fuse burning, fast blink = almost time. Green + slow blink = still lit but out of commission, so the two meanings stay distinct.
+	#
+	# ⚠️ This green is **intentionally never reverted**. The retired device's entire reset is skipped anyway
+	# (no spine_broken_exited_rpc, no set_spine_breaker_rpc(false)),
+	# so it stays green until this match ends — which is exactly the intended effect,
+	# making it obvious at a glance that "the device on that corpse is out of commission and only the other one is still hunting".
+	if v is SpineBreakerPlayer and v.spine_breaker_light != null:
+		v.spine_breaker_light.light_color = MP8_RETIRE_LIGHT
+		if multiplayer.is_server():
+			print("[MP8] Spine Breaker: device %d retired, the light on %s's back turns green" % [mp8_index, String(v.name)])
+
+
